{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.\n\nThe code that sequences the repository's checks carries out the first paragraph.\nA repository-validation function that collects validation errors MUST hold them\nin a parameter or local variable named `errors`, and a function with such a\nvariable is a site below; authoring code that collects errors only to abort is\nnot one. After one of its checks fails, a site MUST still run every later check\nthat does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the\nsame loop iteration, and MUST NOT report one failure twice.\n\n::::{sites} error_accumulators\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nexclude: [src/authoring/inputs.rs, src/authoring/tools.rs]\nrule:\n  kind: function_item\n  has:\n    stopBy: { kind: function_item }\n    any:\n      - kind: parameter\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n      - kind: let_declaration\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n  not:\n    inside:\n      stopBy: end\n      kind: mod_item\n      has: { field: name, regex: \"^tests$\" }\nmatch:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut errors: BTreeSet<String> = BTreeSet::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut errors);\n    }\nno_match:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, discovery_errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut problems = Vec::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut problems);\n    }\n  - |\n    mod tests {\n        fn check(errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\n    }\n```\n::::\n\n::::{violations} early_exits\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nwithin: error_accumulators\nrule:\n  any:\n    - kind: try_expression\n    - kind: return_expression\n    - kind: continue_expression\n    - kind: break_expression\nmatch:\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); continue; }\n            if item.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        if !v.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); return; }\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); break; }\n        }\n        if items.is_empty() { errors.push(\"empty\".into()); }\n    }\nno_match:\n  - |\n    fn parse(v: &Value) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n```\n::::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.ci.errors` has four kinds of obligation:\n1. Validation continues after a failed check and reports every failed check, each as a validation error.\n2. An unparseable file counts as one failed check, and checks that do not depend on its contents still run.\n3. Each site (a function with an `errors` parameter or local) runs every later independent check, including later checks in the same loop iteration, and does not report one failure twice.\n4. The sequencing code carries out the first paragraph.\n\nObligation 3 is upheld by each site and reviewed per site, so I did not vet the sites. Obligations 1, 2 and 4 are carried out by the sequencing code, so they need tests here.\n\n**Tests mapped to obligations**\n- **Continue after failure, and no duplicates.**\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks]` makes name, text, input, definition-resolution and `{fixme}` checks fail together. It asserts that every expected message appears and that the error list has no duplicates.\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors]` and `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[schema_rejects_each_malformed_field_and_input_form]` assert many independent findings in one run. The schema test also asserts that malformed fields suppress only the dependent comparisons.\n  - `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations]` asserts that exactly one error is reported when a site query fails and dependent violation queries exist, in both query orders. It also asserts that an independent query still finds its sites.\n  - `src/index.rs#mod[tests]/fn[an_unreadable_record_is_reported_once]`, `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs#fn[an_unlistable_review_directory_is_reported_once]`, and `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[git_index_failure_is_reported_once_and_later_checks_still_run]` assert once-only reporting of environment failures.\n  - `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once]` and `src/execution_coverage/check.rs#mod[tests]/fn[accepted_exceptions_cannot_waive_invalid_measurement_and_checks_still_collect_errors]` assert no duplicate findings.\n  - `tests/references.rs#fn[malformed_citation_entries_do_not_hide_later_independent_errors]` and `tests/references.rs#fn[unclosed_fences_still_report_unknown_names]` check that later reference and citation findings survive earlier ones.\n- **Parse failures.** `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks]` covers a malformed `habeas.toml` (`version = 2`) and a malformed `book.toml`. Each is reported exactly once, and the test also asserts the independent findings: an invalid evidence file (`evidence/ghost.json`) and an unknown source reference. It also covers `.config/nextest.toml` and coverage-configuration failures. `tests/commands.rs#fn[status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring]` covers a bad `habeas.toml`, including unparseable TOML, with `status` and `info` still reporting a malformed evidence file. `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase]` covers a broken JSON evidence file together with an unparseable Rust source, and asserts that both are reported and that independent coverage reporting continues.\n- **Sequencing.** Every test above goes through `validate_repository`, `inspect_repository` or the CLI, which is the sequencing code. The distributed-requirement mechanics (violation queries need site queries, `within` names) are covered by `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[a_violation_query_needs_a_site_query]` and `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[within_cannot_name_another_violation_query]`.\n\n**Site query.** I ran `requirements.ci.errors/error_accumulators`. It selects the validation and collection functions, with the expected `errors` locals and parameters, across `src` and `crates/habeas-sdk/src`:\n- `src/evidence.rs`: `inspect_repository_with_progress`, `collect_definitions`, `validate_evidence_file`, `validate_input`.\n- `src/citations.rs`: `validate`, `validate_review`.\n- `src/sites.rs`, `src/terms.rs`, `src/rust_coverage.rs`.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/src/access.rs`: `DiscoverySources::files` and `walk`.\n\nSearches for other accumulator names (`findings`, `problems`, `failures`, `diagnostics`, `discovery_errors`) and for `errors` variables in `src/authoring` found no function the query misses. The only non-test authoring functions with an `errors` local are in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` and `src/authoring/tools.rs`. Those are the \"collect only to abort\" cases the `exclude` list names. Test modules are excluded by the `not inside mod tests` clause, and no test-module function appears among the sites. Other `errors`-named locals in tests are not sites. The spec defines a site by the `errors` name, and I found no function that collects validation errors under another name without also having an `errors` variable. `validate_rust_functions` holds `discovery_errors` alongside `errors` and is still selected. I found no out-of-scope selections.\n\n**Query snippets.**\n- The site query's `match` snippets cover a parameter and a local with a non-`Vec` type. Its `no_match` snippets cover a differently named parameter, a differently named local, and a function inside `mod tests`. These show what the query selects and what it must not.\n- The violation query `early_exits` has `match` snippets for `?`, `continue`, `return` and `break`, each followed by a later check. It has a `no_match` snippet for the same construct in a function with no `errors` variable. That is adequate, and a violation query that misses constructs would not be a gap anyway.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. Every obligation is either upheld per site or carried out by sequencing code with asserting tests, and the queries are adequately exercised by their snippets.",
